 /*
  * Erbium xSPI target interface model
  *
- * See include/hw/ssi/erbium-xspi.h and docs/erbium-qemu-review.md in the
- * erbium-emu repository for the architecture.
- *
  * Physical wiring being modelled: the host's OSPI controller talks to the
- * Erbium xSPI target IP over CS/DQ[7:0]/RWDS.  Inside the chip that IP owns
- * the SCCR register block (reached with Read/Write Register commands) and is
- * an AXI4 initiator on the NIC (Read/Write Memory commands).  Here:
+ * Erbium xSPI target IP over CS#/DQ[7:0]/RWDS.  Inside the chip that IP owns
+ * the SCCR register block (Read/Write Register commands, and memory ops at
+ * 0x4000F000) and is an AXI4 initiator on the NoC (Read/Write Memory
+ * commands).  Here:
  *   - MRAM is a host memory backend (shared mmap file) so a co-running
  *     erbium_emu CPU model sees the same bytes;
- *   - non-MRAM memory accesses are forwarded over a chardev socket to
- *     erbium_emu, which models the NIC and its targets (docs/protocol.md);
- *     without a chardev, a small built-in stub answers instead.
+ *   - other NoC targets are reached over a chardev socket to erbium_emu
+ *     (docs/protocol.md); without a chardev a small built-in stub answers.
+ *
+ * Semantics follow the RTL (ip/xspi/bsv/xspi.bsv); the notable ones:
+ *   - latency for every read = 8 + CFG.InitialLatency SCK cycles;
+ *   - one AXI read burst per CS#: 8 bytes unless CFG.BurstEnable, then
+ *     128/64/16/32 bytes for BurstLength 0..3; further bytes read 0 and set
+ *     interrupt_status.read_underflow;
+ *   - writes are 8-byte single-beat AXI writes, address increments within
+ *     a 4 KiB page, trailing partial words are dropped;
+ *   - register ops are 32-bit at SCCR offset addr[5:0], never incremented;
+ *   - SFDP DWORDs are streamed with 4 zero pad bytes each (RTL quirk);
+ *   - the opcode extension byte is present in S4/D4/S8/D8 command rates and
+ *     its value is ignored; 99h is a whole-chip soft reset.
  *
  * This work is licensed under the terms of the GNU GPL, version 2 or later.
  */
